{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_01Gt5cEvQ9Rwy7RSSzddcFhH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The inline template slots (Title / Anchors / Member documents) arrived empty, but the actual session artifact is on disk: `report.md` is the researchPrime trio run on grass-4199 \"Improved Foreman (make parallel).\" Summarizing that as grounded bullet claims, each tagged with its evidence grounding.\n\n## Research trio session â€” \"Can Foreman run builds in parallel to cut wall-clock?\" (grass-4199)\n\n**Bottom line**\n- Parallelism is **mostly the wrong lever** for the stated goal (cut wall-clock without losing integrity). Write-parallelism loses for *two independent, over-determined* reasons. *[adjudicated conclusion]*\n- **Conflict-safety:** parallel writers collide on **semantic interface contracts**, not just files â€” a file-diff disjointness check is blind to this (one wave adds `grass_origin=\"\"`, another assumes `None`). A single sequential author *is* the guarantee. *[OBSERVED in real plans]*\n- **Quota topology (decisive):** every model step draws from one Pro/Max window (~1â€“3 waves/window, no live quota API). N parallel agents drain it NÃ— faster, so `wall_clock_floor â‰ˆ total_tokens / window_rate`. Adding model calls to buy latency can **net-slow** a quota-bound run. Anthropic's \"15Ã— tokens for 90% faster\" is a *metered-API* result that inverts on fixed quota. *[CLAIMED/reasoned from observed quota behavior]*\n\n**What actually delays a deliverable**\n- **No wall-clock data exists to optimize yet** â€” logs record only dates + fix-iteration counts; dashboard `elapsed`/`token%` are hardcoded `null`; 54 waves were ~same-day. STEP ZERO = instrument per-phase elapsed/tokens (`transport.mjs` already logs `duration_ms`/`output_tokens`; nothing aggregates them). *[OBSERVED]*\n- **Calendar time is dominated by human approval gates, not machine time** â€” Crucible has 3 standing HALTs; Anchor adds per-UI-wave sign-offs; v6â€“v11 sat on branches for days. Foreman itself auto-advances with zero scheduled human gates. *[single-origin but well-grounded in dated STATUS lines]*\n\n**Ranked recommendations**\n- **SHIP â€” concurrent read-only reviewers** (`forâ€¦await` â†’ `Promise.allSettled` at `wave-engine.mjs:861`): ~1.2â€“1.3Ã— tail-latency win, robustness preserved (no commit/checkpoint change; merge is order-independent by stable-id). Still draws RÃ— tokens. *[OBSERVED-grounded]*\n- **SHIP â€” reword the topology rule** to \"No parallel agent may MUTATE a shared, under-specified decision surface\" as an unconditional bright line (accuracy fix, strengthens the rule, no cost). *[adjudicated]*\n- **MEASURE â€” overlap review with the off-quota `spawnSync` test gate** (additive only if the gate is slow vs. review; no extra model calls).\n- **PURSUE (non-parallel) â€” reduce mean fix-iterations *k*** (better EXECUTE prompt / stronger EXECUTE model): ~33% win (k 2â†’1), *fewer* tokens.\n- **PURSUE with guards â€” cheaper/faster second reviewer:** ~17â€“22% win, halves review tokens; gated by Q1/Q2 below.\n- **VALID (different goal) â€” parallelism across independent projects** (separate plans/repos/checkpoints): real throughput win, zero merge risk; `worktrees.py` isolation already fits.\n- **DO NOT SHIP â€” wave-DAG / worktree-band parallelism:** only ~1.2â€“1.6Ã— on the minority of plans with independent bands, and N commit chains forfeit the single-checkpoint/single-branch resume guarantee. *[OBSERVED: no merge machinery exists; Crucible `dependsOn` is single linear predecessor, populated by zero real plans]*\n- **REJECT â€” speculative/parallel EXECUTE & cross-wave pipelining** (violates the ascending truth-gate; ~2Ã— tokens when quota-bound) and **shrinking the gate to \"impacted tests only\"** (it *is* the vacuous-GREEN attack the full-suite gate exists to close).\n- **Adjudicated:** an affected-tests pre-check is OK as an inner-loop accelerator only â€” **the gate of record stays the full suite.**\n\n**Locked planning requirements (the cheaper-reviewer lever)**\n- **Q1 â€” \"good enough?\":** the reviewer is *not* the correctness arbiter â€” the orchestrator-run full-suite test gate is; reviewers are an adversarial supplement. Keep ONE reviewer strong, make only the SECOND cheap (a BLOCKER needs â‰¥2 agreement, so a cheap second can only fail to confirm, never raise a false blocker alone). Promote only on a measured **recall** metric (shadow period + held-out historical bad diffs), not a vibe.\n- **Q2 â€” \"could it get stuck on a low tier?\":** never hard-code a model id â€” resolve by **tier/policy** at run time (same discipline as `REVIEWER_COUNT`); add a staleness guard + scheduled re-calibration that re-runs the Q1 recall check on a model-release trigger.\n\n**Honesty stamps / caveats**\n- Mode: ENGINE-capable (Node probe GO, 28 trio symbols), Phase-3 run via **real fresh-context sub-agents** applying engine governance â€” *not* the in-process scoring path. Isolation: real (parallel-swarm host).\n- **cross_model: false** â€” single model family across the 3 reviewers (correlated-blind-spot risk); mitigated by grounding in OBSERVED source + the independent prior study `foreman-review-2026-06-19`.\n- **Citation integrity:** Anthropic + Cognition are 2 genuinely independent origins (not a bandwagon), but *neither* has OBSERVED parallel-code-writing data â€” that sub-claim is CORROBORATED-by-opinion, not OBSERVED. SKILL.md over-generalizes by citing Anthropic (whose own system parallelizes read-only research) as an anti-parallel witness; the supportable claim is the narrower shared-mutation one.\n\n**Net:** Foreman stays single-threaded for all write work. Ship concurrent reviewers + the reword. The biggest real \"time-to-deliverable\" wins are *non-parallel*: instrument first, cut fix-iterations, overlap the off-quota gate, and run independent projects in parallel for more deliverables/day.\n\n---\n\nTwo notes on